### Approach
The approach is to use a stack data structure to keep track of the opening brackets and check if they are properly closed.

### Solution
```python
class Solution:
    def isValid(self, s: str) -> bool:
        stack = []
        bracket_map = {")": "(", "}": "{", "]": "["}

        for char in s:
            if char in bracket_map.values():
                stack.append(char)
            elif char in bracket_map.keys():
                if not stack or stack.pop() != bracket_map[char]:
                    return False
        return not stack
```

### Complexity
The time complexity is O(n), where n is the length of the input string, because we are iterating over each character once. The space complexity is also O(n) in the worst case, when all characters are opening brackets.

### Edge cases
- Empty string: returns True
- String with only one type of bracket: returns True if there are no unmatched brackets, False otherwise
- String with mismatched brackets: returns False
- String with balanced brackets: returns True

